{"kind":"artifact","text":".github/scripts at exact clean pushed head 33d6126ffecadace3a501474336e18313b848264 on request/repo-security-gates-current, fulfilling codex's c488f287 under promise ce7c1752. It closes both defects in codex's 9da9c4f9, which I ratified.\n\nTHE SCANNER NO LONGER PRINTS WHAT IT FINDS. It passed the whole git grep match to report, so a detected credential was echoed verbatim into the CI log of a public repository: the tool whose purpose is to stop a secret reaching a public place published it to one on success. Both defects are cured by the same structural move rather than by filtering the output afterwards -- every invocation is git grep -l, so only file names ever cross from git into the script and no code path can print content, whatever anyone adds later. The report names the file and the provider pattern that matched.\n\nAND IT NO LONGER SKIPS WHAT IT CLAIMED TO SCAN. The -I flag omitted binary-classified files while the header claimed the tracked tree without qualification, so a credential in a tracked file with a NUL byte in it was missed while the run reported success -- a clean result nobody had reason to doubt. Dropping -I widens the scan to that content; with -l the cost stays one linear pass per pattern per blob and nothing streams through the shell, which the old -n design would have done with a multi-megabyte binary line. The header now states both properties instead of the one that was untrue.\n\nTHE SUITE WAS BLIND TO THE FIRST DEFECT BY CONSTRUCTION. All thirteen cases asserted detect-or-pass and none asserted anything about the output. There are fifteen now: one plants a credential in a NUL-containing tracked file, one plants all six shapes and greps the combined output for each, and every existing detect case now also asserts the planted content is absent and the file is named.\n\nGATES I RAN AT THIS EXACT HEAD: the scanner's own suite, all fifteen cases; the scanner over this repository, clean; gofmt clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... green across every package; go mod verify all modules verified; GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build ./... clean; git diff --check clean; worktree clean; origin equals the head.\n\nTHREE MUTATIONS I RAN MYSELF, restoring to a recorded sha256 baseline after each and confirming the hash matched. Putting the matched text back into the report -- git grep -n and reporting the hit -- kills seven cases, including 'output is redacted: a planted credential appears in scanner output', which is the defect itself. Restoring -I kills exactly one, 'binary-classified file: a credential in a NUL-containing tracked file was missed (status 0)', and it is worth reading that failure twice: the scanner reported a clean tree it had never looked at. Setting status=0 in report kills every detect case.","body":{"commit":"33d6126ffecadace3a501474336e18313b848264","path":".github/scripts"}}